\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Horizon redshift $z_{\mathrm  {horizon}}$ as a function of chirp mass $\mathcal  {M}_c$. The scattered points represent the simulated detectable binary black hole events, while the solid curve shows the estimated horizon redshift for each chirp mass. The horizon redshift increases with chirp mass up to approximately $180\,M_\odot $, before decreasing at higher masses, reflecting the mass-dependent sensitivity of gravitational-wave detectors.}}{16}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:z_hor_vs_chirp_mass}{{2}{16}{Horizon redshift $z_{\mathrm {horizon}}$ as a function of chirp mass $\mathcal {M}_c$. The scattered points represent the simulated detectable binary black hole events, while the solid curve shows the estimated horizon redshift for each chirp mass. The horizon redshift increases with chirp mass up to approximately $180\,M_\odot $, before decreasing at higher masses, reflecting the mass-dependent sensitivity of gravitational-wave detectors}{figure.2}{}}
