\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Logarithm of the selection-correction weight as a function of chirp mass $\mathcal  {M}_c$, calculated from the expected number of detectable mergers within the horizon redshift. The weight decreases sharply with increasing chirp mass at lower masses, reaching a minimum around $180$--$200\,M_\odot $, before increasing gradually at higher masses. Larger weights correspond to systems with lower detection efficiency and therefore a greater probability of being retained during the rejection sampling used to reconstruct the astrophysical population.}}{17}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:log_weight_vs_chirp_mass}{{3}{17}{Logarithm of the selection-correction weight as a function of chirp mass $\mathcal {M}_c$, calculated from the expected number of detectable mergers within the horizon redshift. The weight decreases sharply with increasing chirp mass at lower masses, reaching a minimum around $180$--$200\,M_\odot $, before increasing gradually at higher masses. Larger weights correspond to systems with lower detection efficiency and therefore a greater probability of being retained during the rejection sampling used to reconstruct the astrophysical population}{figure.3}{}}
